<p>
  Give your customers and suppliers their own login to see the documents you share with them,
  and to respond without emails going back and forth.
</p>

<h2>Inviting someone</h2>
<p>
  Open the customer or supplier (a contact or company), find <strong>Portal access</strong>, enter
  their email and choose the <em>customer</em>
  or <em>supplier</em>
  portal. They get an email with a link; they sign in (or create a login) with that email address
  and land in your portal. Revoke access at any time from the same card.
</p>

<h2>What customers can do</h2>
<ul>
  <li>See the quotes, orders and invoices you've sent them (never drafts), and what they owe.</li>
  <li>
    <strong>Accept</strong>
    or <strong>decline</strong>
    a quote — you see the answer straight away.
  </li>
  <li>Print or save any document as PDF.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What suppliers can do</h2>
<ul>
  <li>
    Answer a <strong>request for quotation</strong>
    with their unit prices and reference; it's marked as quoted for you.
  </li>
  <li><strong>Acknowledge</strong> purchase orders, so you know they were received.</li>
  <li>Follow their bills and whether they've been paid.</li>
</ul>
<.callout>
  Portal users only ever see documents for their own company with you — nothing else in your
  workspace.
</.callout>
